'''''Palafoxia callosa''''', commonly known as the '''small palafox''',<ref>{{PLANTS|id=PACA3|taxon=Palafoxia callosa|accessdate=2 January 2018}}</ref> or '''spanish needles'''<ref name=MissouriPlants /> is a species of flowering plant in the [[Asteraceae|aster family]].

It is native to North America, where it is found in the south-central United States and in the state of [[Coahuila]] in Mexico.<ref>{{BONAP|ref|genus=Palafoxia|species=callosa|accessdate=2 January 2018}}</ref><ref name=fna>[http://www.efloras.org/florataxon.aspx?flora_id=1&taxon_id=242416916 Flora of North America, ''Palafoxia callosa'']</ref> A disjunct population is found in the [[Black Belt (geological formation)|Blackland Prairie]] region of [[Mississippi]] in the United States.<ref name=Weakley>{{cite web |url= http://www.herbarium.unc.edu/flora.htm |title= Flora of the Southern and Mid-Atlantic States |first1= Alan |last1= Weakley |author1-link= Alan S. Weakley |date= 2015 |access-date= 2018-01-03 |archive-date= 2018-10-06 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20181006082209/http://herbarium.unc.edu/flora.htm |url-status= dead }}</ref> Its natural habitat is rocky, calcareous soil in glades and prairies. It is less commonly found along the banks of streams.<ref name=Missouri>{{cite book |last=Yatskievych |first=George |author-link= George Yatskievych|date=2006 |title=Flora of Missouri, Volume 2 |url= |location= |publisher=Missouri Botanical Garden Press |pages=524 |isbn= 9781930723498  }}</ref><ref name=Weakley/> It is popular for [[Butterfly gardening|butterfly gardens]].<ref name=MissouriPlants />

''Palafoxia callosa'' is an annual. It produces heads of pink to white flowers in late summer and fall.<ref name=Missouri/> Its narrow leaves are often wilted by flowering time.<ref name=MissouriPlants>{{cite web|url=https://www.missouriplants.com/Palafoxia_callosa_page.html |title= Palafoxia callosa |website=MissouriPlants}}</ref>

